    <string name="article_thirst_title">Increased thirst and drinking</string>
    <string name="article_thirst_summary">Drinking more than usual can point to kidney disease, diabetes, hyperthyroidism, and other issues. If intake stays high — especially over ~100 ml/kg/day — see a vet. Never withhold water.</string>
    <string name="article_drink_wee_title">Is my cat drinking and weeing too much?</string>
    <string name="article_drink_wee_summary">More drinking and more weeing often travel together. Common causes include urine infection, kidney disease, diabetes, and hyperthyroidism. Take a urine sample to the vet if you can.</string>
    <string name="article_diabetes_title">Feline diabetes</string>
    <string name="article_diabetes_summary">Classic signs include weight loss despite a good appetite, plus increased thirst and urination. Only a vet can diagnose and plan treatment — this is background reading, not a label for your cat.</string>
    <string name="article_hyperthyroid_title">Hyperthyroidism in cats</string>
    <string name="article_hyperthyroid_summary">Common in older cats: weight loss, bigger appetite, more thirst and urination, sometimes vomiting or a scruffy coat. Vet bloodwork is needed to confirm.</string>
    <string name="article_vomiting_title">Vomiting in cats</string>
    <string name="article_vomiting_summary">Occasional hairballs differ from repeated vomiting with a poor appetite. Repeated vomiting is a reason to contact a vet promptly.</string>
    <string name="article_emergency_title">When pet symptoms need urgent care</string>
    <string name="article_emergency_summary">Sudden collapse, straining without urine, severe lethargy, or ongoing vomiting can be emergencies. If you\'re unsure, err on the side of calling a vet or emergency clinic.</string>
